concept identifiers {
  name = "Identifiers"
  summary = "The naming rule for fields, types, kinds, labels, and bindings, plus reserved words."
  related = [{ id: "symbols", why: "Symbols supports Identifiers: Identifier-like :name values for tags and enum-like choices, plus symbol_set vocabularies." }]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p <<DOC
      Identifiers are the names used throughout WCL — for fields, types, block kinds, block labels,
      variants, symbols, `let` bindings, and imported items. The lexical rule is the same
      everywhere, with one convenience exception for block labels.
    DOC
    h2 "Lexical rule"
    p <<DOC
      An identifier starts with an **ASCII letter** (`a`-`z`, `A`-`Z`) or an **underscore**, and
      continues with letters, digits, or underscores. No Unicode, no dashes, no spaces.
    DOC
    table {
      header = ["Name", "Legal?", "Why"]
      rows = [["`name`, `my_field`, `_internal`", "yes", "Letters and underscores"], ["`v2`, `HTTPStatus`", "yes", "Digits and capitals are fine after the first character"], ["`2nd_attempt`", "no", "Must not start with a digit"], ["`kebab-case`", "no", "Dashes are not identifier characters — except in block labels, below"]]
    }
    h2 "Block labels: kebab-case and paths"
    p <<DOC
      Block **labels** (the name after a block kind) are the one place a bare identifier may contain
      `-` and `/` connectors — so kebab-case class names and path-like page names need no quoting.
      The connector sits directly between name parts (no surrounding spaces).
    DOC
    code "wcl" {
      source = <<DOC
        class dgm-box {}              // kebab-case, bare
        class wdoc-series-1 {}        // trailing number is fine
        page reference/intro {}       // path-like
        page api/v1/users {}

        class "dgm-box" {}            // quoting still works
      DOC
    }
    h2 "Reserved words"
    p <<DOC
      A handful of words are reserved by the lexer and cannot be used as identifiers in any
      position:
    DOC
    table {
      header = ["Reserved", "Used for"]
      rows = [["`true`, `false`", "Boolean literals"], ["`none`", "The none literal"], ["`if`, `else`, `match`", "Control flow"]]
    }
    p <<DOC
      Other words that look special — `type`, `interface`, `union`, `symbol_set`, `let`, `import`,
      `connection`, `fn`, `extends`, `as` — are recognised only in declaration positions, so they
      may also appear as ordinary identifiers. It reads more clearly to keep them for their
      declaration use.
    DOC
    h2 "Naming conventions"
    p <<DOC
      The standard library follows Rust-like conventions, but the language does not enforce them.
      Pick a style and stay consistent.
    DOC
    table {
      header = ["Convention", "Used for"]
      rows = [["`snake_case`", "Fields, `let` bindings, block kinds, symbols"], ["`PascalCase`", "Types, interfaces, unions, variants, symbol sets"], ["`SCREAMING`", "Rarely; reserve for visibly-constant globals"]]
    }
  }
}
